faqs:
  - q: "What is an EMI (Equated Monthly Installment)?"
    a: "An Equated Monthly Installment (EMI) is a fixed monthly payment made by a borrower to a lender on a specified calendar date. Each installment covers both interest accrued for the month and a portion of the principal balance."
  - q: "What is the standard formula for calculating loan EMI?"
    a: "The standard EMI formula is: EMI = P × r × (1 + r)^n / ((1 + r)^n - 1), where P is the loan principal, r is the periodic monthly interest rate (annual rate divided by 1200), and n is the total number of monthly payments."
  - q: "How does paying extra each month shorten loan duration?"
    a: "Any payment above the required EMI is credited entirely against the loan's outstanding principal balance. With a smaller principal, future monthly interest charges decrease, accelerating repayment and saving substantial interest charges."
  - q: "Does this loan calculator support multiple international currencies?"
    a: "Yes. You can switch between PKR, INR, USD, EUR, GBP, AED, and SAR without changing the mathematical annuity formula."
  - q: "Can I export my full amortization schedule?"
    a: "Yes. Click the 'Download Schedule (CSV)' button to export your complete month-by-month payment, interest, principal, and balance schedule as a CSV spreadsheet."
  - q: "Is my personal loan data uploaded to any server?"
    a: "No. All loan computations execute 100% locally in your web browser. No financial numbers or loan amounts are transmitted or saved."
sources:
  - label: "Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) - Loan Basics"
    url: "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/"
  - label: "Federal Reserve Board - Consumer Guide to Mortgages"
    url: "https://www.federalreserve.gov/"
  - label: "Bank for International Settlements - Credit Standards"
    url: "https://www.bis.org/"
